Short:        CopperNova CNP2 filesystem preview
Author:       Ilkka Lehtoranta
Uploader:     ilkleht@gmail.com (Ilkka Lehtoranta)
Type:         disk/misc
Version:      1.0-preview.4
Requires:     AmigaOS 2.04, 68000 or newer, TD64-capable disk driver
Architecture: m68k-amigaos >= 2.04

CopperNova is a new MIT-licensed Amiga filesystem written in C#.
This preview uses CNP2 root version 3 with wide extents and multi-group
allocation. The disk driver must support TD64 reads and writes and
CMD_UPDATE for Flush, including on partitions below 4 GiB.
It supports creating, deleting and moving files and directories, file
reads and writes, enumeration, and explicit Flush.

Archive contents

CopperNovaPrototype  Native 68000 CNP2 handler
CopperNovaFormat     Amiga Shell command for initializing a CNP2 partition
CopperNova.readme    This README
LICENSE.txt         MIT license
THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt  CopperSharp license

Installation

1. Use HDToolBox or your controller's RDB tool on a spare disk or image.
   Create a fresh, empty partition larger than 2 MiB with 512-byte
   filesystem blocks.
   Leave Bootable off. Use your controller's Mask and MaxTransfer values.
2. In HDToolBox, open Partition Drive, enable Advanced Options and use
   Add/Update to add CopperNovaPrototype to the RDB. Set its identifier
   to 0x434E5032 (CNP2) and version/revision to 1/0 if asked. Under
   Change, select the newly added filesystem or set that same identifier
   as the custom filesystem type for the partition. Save and reboot.
3. From an Amiga Shell, run the formatter with the partition's device
   name and the INITIALIZE keyword. For a partition named CNP0:

       CopperNovaFormat CNP0: INITIALIZE

   Use this command only on a new, empty partition. The command requires
   both CNP2 root sectors to be zero and refuses a mounted CNP2 volume.
   After successful initialization you can use CNP0:. The display
   volume name in this preview is CopperNovaPrototype.

The optional CopperNovaDemo archive contains a preformatted emulator
hardfile. Converting another filesystem in place is outside this preview.

Current limitations

- 512-byte sectors. The demo partition is 4 GiB + 64 MiB.
  Larger geometries and physical controllers have yet to be qualified.
- Up to 1,022 live objects. Names are ASCII, with up to 168 bytes per
  component and a 255-byte DOS path. Each directory has 192 bytes for
  name records, which can limit it to a few entries with long names.
- Up to eight extents and 2 GiB minus one byte per file. A fragmented file
  can reach the extent limit before the disk fills.
- Links, sparse growth, grow-by-resize, extended attributes, notifications,
  record locking, automatic TRIM and removable-media change handling are
  outside this preview.
- Writes can be buffered. Use a clean shutdown or a successful explicit
  Flush before removing the volume. An interrupted in-place overwrite
  before Flush can leave a partially updated payload.
- Experimental images may need to be recreated for future releases.
  Keep independent copies of your files.

The preview was tested with a fresh HDToolBox install on Kickstart
2.04, including TD64 writes across 4 GiB, explicit Flush, and recovery
after simulated power cuts. Physical hardware and storage qualification
remain open.

Source code is at https://github.com/ilehtoranta/CopperFS
